      id: 'l12', key: 'nc-gr-12', title: 'Redundant Connection', concept: 'al-redundant-connection', level: 'Medium',
      story: "A tree of n nodes had one extra edge added, creating exactly one cycle. Return the edge that can be removed to restore a tree (the last such edge in the input). With **union-find**, the first edge whose endpoints are already connected is the culprit.",
      analogy: "Laying cables between buildings one at a time: the first cable connecting two buildings that are already linked is the redundant one.",
      approach: "Process edges in order with union-find. For [a, b], if find(a) === find(b), return [a, b]; otherwise union them. Nodes are labelled 1..n.",
      brute: ['remove each edge and test whether the rest is a tree', "// For each edge from last to first, drop it and check (with DFS) that the remaining graph is connected and acyclic.\n// O(E · (V + E))."],
      best: ['union-find: first edge closing a cycle', "function findRedundantConnection(edges) {\n  const parent = Array.from({ length: edges.length + 1 }, (_, i) => i);\n  const find = (x) => (parent[x] === x ? x : (parent[x] = find(parent[x])));\n  for (const [a, b] of edges) {\n    const ra = find(a), rb = find(b);\n    if (ra === rb) return [a, b];\n    parent[ra] = rb;\n  }\n  return [];\n}\nconsole.log(findRedundantConnection([[1, 2], [1, 3], [2, 3]]));", '[ 2, 3 ]'],
      cx: [['Remove and recheck', 'O(E · (V + E))', 'O(V + E)'], ['Union-find', 'O(E · α(V))', 'O(V)']],
      tip: 'Size the parent array n + 1 when nodes are labelled from 1.',
      warn: 'The directed version of this problem (Redundant Connection II) is harder: a node can also end up with two parents.',
      faq: [['Why is the first cycle-closing edge the answer?', 'Edges before it form a forest; it is the one that creates the cycle, and with exactly one extra edge it is the last edge on that cycle in input order.'], ['What is the complexity?', 'Nearly linear: O(E · α(V)).']],
      ex: { prompt: 'Write findRedundantConnection(edges) for a graph on nodes 1..n that was a tree plus one extra edge. Return the edge that, when processed in order, first connects two already-connected nodes.', py: "def findRedundantConnection(edges):\n    parent = list(range(len(edges) + 1))\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    for a, b in edges:\n        ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n        if ra == rb:\n            return [a, b]\n        parent[ra] = rb\n    return []\n", sig: "(int[][] edges) -> int[]", java: "class Solution {\n    private int[] parent;\n    public int[] findRedundantConnection(int[][] edges) {\n        parent = new int[edges.length + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i < parent.length; i++) parent[i] = i;\n        for (int[] e : edges) {\n            int a = find(e[0]), b = find(e[1]);\n            if (a == b) return e;\n            parent[a] = b;\n        }\n        return new int[0];\n    }\n    private int find(int x) {\n        return parent[x] == x ? x : (parent[x] = find(parent[x]));\n    }\n}\n", cpp: "class Solution {\npublic:\n    vector<int> findRedundantConnection(vector<vector<int>>& edges) {\n        parent.resize(edges.size() + 1);\n        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);\n        for (auto& e : edges) {\n            int a = find(e[0]), b = find(e[1]);\n            if (a == b) return e;\n            parent[a] = b;\n        }\n        return {};\n    }\nprivate:\n    vector<int> parent;\n    int find(int x) { return parent[x] == x ? x : parent[x] = find(parent[x]); }\n};\n", fn: 'findRedundantConnection', params: 'edges', cases: [{ args: [[[1, 2], [1, 3], [2, 3]]], expect: [2, 3] }, { args: [[[1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4], [1, 4], [1, 5]]], expect: [1, 4] }], solution: 'function findRedundantConnection(e){const p=Array.from({length:e.length+1},(_,i)=>i);const f=x=>p[x]===x?x:(p[x]=f(p[x]));for(const [a,b] of e){const ra=f(a),rb=f(b);if(ra===rb)return[a,b];p[ra]=rb}return[]}', hint: 'Union-find: return the edge whose endpoints already share a root.' },
